{
  "requirement": "A {term}`requirements.references.definition_selection` in a requirements input MUST continue to select requirements and named examples, excluding named definitions. Definitions referenced by selected texts MUST be captured as separate definition inputs, regardless of whether their names match the selection. Sharing a selection's matching name prefix alone MUST NOT make a definition a dependency.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment: the implementation and tests satisfy `requirements.definitions.selection`.**\n\n**Implementation**\n- **Selection excludes named definitions.** `InputSelector::resolve` for `Requirements` (src/inputs.rs) skips every `DefinitionKind::Term`. It matches the rest with `definition_glob::matches`, so a full name without `*` selects exactly that definition. An empty match set is an error: \"matches no requirement or example\".\n- **Same rule on every selection path.**\n  - `source_input` (src/authoring/inputs.rs) applies the same Term filter when it checks read access.\n  - `status_markdown_with_options` and `info_markdown` (src/commands.rs) skip Terms and report \"no definitions match\" when nothing is left.\n  - `add_input` and `update_inputs` (src/add_input.rs) reach the same `resolve`.\n  - `validate_resolved_input` (src/evidence.rs) re-resolves recorded inputs through it, so validation applies it too.\n  - `preprocess_book` is marked `{impl}`. Its book rendering keeps Terms out of the ordered list of requirements and examples (`order`), so they never appear as requirements or examples.\n- **Referenced definitions are captured as separate inputs.**\n  - `terms::direct_references` scans the bodies of the selected `Requirements` text for `{term}` references. It also scans the subject text, definition inputs and outline inputs.\n  - `close_dependencies` expands the references transitively to a fixed point.\n  - `dependencies` returns the resulting set.\n  - `update_inputs` adds each one as its own `Definition` input, whether or not its name matches the selection glob.\n  - `definition_inputs` does the same for authoring.\n  - `validate_dependencies` requires exactly one definition input per dependency.\n- **A shared name prefix is not a dependency.** Dependencies come only from actual `{term}` references in the text. Nothing derives them from glob or prefix matches.\n- **Marking.** All the relevant code carries `{impl}` markers. `additional_definitions` is unmarked for this requirement but delegates to the marked `definition_inputs`. The `references` tool (src/authoring/tools.rs) also filters out Terms. It handles a CLI-style discovery selection rather than a \"requirements input\", and it is marked for `definition_globs`. I found no unmarked behavior that this requirement specifies, and no behavior in the `{impl}` code outside this requirement and the other requirements it implements.\n\n**Tests**\n- `selections_match_requirements_and_examples_but_never_named_definitions` (tests/definitions.rs):\n  - The glob `rules.*` matches the requirement and the example but not the same-prefix definition `rules.used`.\n  - `rules.used` is not captured as a dependency.\n  - `add_input` with the exact name of a definition fails with \"matches no requirement or example\".\n  - `habeas status rules.*` and `habeas info rules.*` list the requirement and example but not the definition.\n  - `habeas info rules.used` fails.\n- `globs_exclude_terms_but_capture_referenced_terms_outside_the_glob` (tests/definitions.rs):\n  - The recorded input's text contains no `{definition}` block.\n  - Inputs after the first are exactly `glossary.a`, `glossary.b` (transitive) and `rules.used`, the referenced definitions, including one outside the `rules.*` glob.\n  - `rules.unrelated`, which shares the prefix but is not referenced, is absent from the inputs and from validation errors.\n- `requirements_reads_select_requirements_and_examples_but_not_definitions` (src/authoring/tools.rs): the `habeas` read tool returns matches `[requirements.case, requirements.ready]`, with no definition among them. Reading a definition by its exact name through the `requirements` form fails.\n\nThese cover the boundaries: glob versus exact name, prefix-sharing definitions, transitive dependencies, dependencies outside the glob, and the failure case of an empty selection. No gaps are acknowledged by FIXME.",
  "references": "2f245a0a2a79fa55401b14b2ff61f57c0953d41d08e781ce65ef052194505e72",
  "inputs": [
    {
      "requirements": "requirements.definitions.selection",
